F12 (HIGH): `gh run download` stages the artifact zip in the OS temp dir before extracting. On this machine /tmp is a 512 MB tmpfs, so any artifact over ~200 MB dies with `error writing zip archive: write /tmp/gh-artifact.<n>.zip: no space left on device` (observed verbatim from the monitor's job log for the 302 MB board chunk). The destination directory itself is on the data disk with tens of GB free — only the temp staging is starved.

Fix: in monitor(), before the artifact download call, create a staging directory on the data disk — path.join(repoDir, '.heart-tmp') (repoDir is already a monitor parameter; create it with fs.mkdirSync recursive) — and pass the download call an env that merges process.env with TMPDIR set to that staging directory, keeping the existing 30-minute timeout. (gh is a Go binary; Go's os.TempDir honors TMPDIR.) Apply this to the single download call in monitor() — it serves both the stills and assembly kinds. Do not change any other gh() call's environment.

